# Normalize line endings to LF on commit
* text=auto eol=lf
# Explicitly enforce LF for source files
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.jsx  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
# Binary files — no line ending conversion
*.png  binary
*.webp binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary

# GPX test fixtures are byte-level inputs, not source.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` above rewrites them on the way in AND on the way out, so a
# fixture cannot carry the bytes it exists to test. bom-crlf.gpx is a Windows
# export — UTF-8 BOM plus CRLF — and collection-gpx.helpers.test.ts checks both
# before using it ("the fixture must really be what it claims, or this case tests
# nothing"). With the conversion active the CRLF is stripped at commit time, the
# guard fails, and the case silently stops covering CRLF input.
#
# Both attributes have to be turned off: `-text` stops the content sniffing, but
# the `eol=lf` inherited from the first line still rewrites line endings on its
# own, so `-eol` is what actually keeps the bytes (`-text -eol` == binary to git
# for this path, without pretending the file is unmergeable).
*.gpx -text -eol
